And this should be posted later also. Would

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:46.300
>> Yes, also posted on the town website if anyone would like to view it during the meeting. Yeah. Okay thanks Amanda. Okay

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.420
so

00:05:52.525 --> 00:05:53.965
>> I won't advance.

00:05:54.605 --> 00:05:55.245
Okay,

00:05:55.405 --> 00:05:55.965
so

00:05:56.285 --> 00:06:02.845
I just wanted to start talking a little bit about the history of where we got to where we are now.

00:06:03.565 --> 00:06:05.965
In a normal budget year in December,

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:06.445
all the

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.070
department heads submit a budget

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:15.670
for their department to myself as the administrator and the board of selectmen.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.470
I collate all that and with

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.545
the treasurer Barbara,

00:06:21.625 --> 00:06:24.265
we put it into one coherent document.

00:06:24.585 --> 00:06:27.145
So I submitted in January,

00:06:27.225 --> 00:06:28.585
at the end of January,

00:06:28.905 --> 00:06:30.985
a set of capital recommendations

00:06:30.985 --> 00:06:32.745
to the Board of Selectmen,

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.145
as well as a preliminary town budget.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:36.550
At

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.670
roughly the same time, the RAM Board of Education

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:45.190
presented a preliminary budget to the region and Andover Elementary School

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.990
also presented a preliminary budget.

00:06:48.875 --> 00:06:50.315
On February

00:06:50.315 --> 00:06:51.355
26,

00:06:51.595 --> 00:06:53.355
the Board of Selectmen

00:06:54.155 --> 00:06:58.075
motioned to send the budget to the Board of Finance.

00:06:58.395 --> 00:07:01.595
The initial budget they proposed had a 12%

00:07:01.595 --> 00:07:02.155
increase

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:03.740
in the final,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:09.420
in the mill rate and would have resulted in a mill rate of about 39.2.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.860
This was when we were still assuming it was going to the voters

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:14.460
to be viewed.

00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:16.885
In March,

00:07:17.285 --> 00:07:20.805
as all of you are well aware, COVID-nineteen

00:07:21.045 --> 00:07:21.845
changed

00:07:22.485 --> 00:07:25.045
a lot of things in the way we live

00:07:25.525 --> 00:07:28.885
and it impacted the town, the state, and the world.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.200
Both the state and town declared a public health emergency

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.240
and on March 21,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.000
Governor Lamont

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.040
made an executive order that requires

00:07:41.615 --> 00:07:47.535
the Board of Finance to pass a budget and set a mill rate and bypasses

00:07:47.535 --> 00:07:49.135
all the normal budgetary

00:07:49.135 --> 00:07:50.015
process

00:07:50.175 --> 00:07:52.895
or much of the normal budgetary process

00:07:53.215 --> 00:07:54.015
and

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:58.340
the ability for town residents to vote on the budget.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:00.580
And just recently,

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:14.705
the governor passed a second or made a second executive order, which basically said, yes, when I issued that order on March 21, I wasn't kidding, you really have to do it. It's not optional.

00:08:15.345 --> 00:08:16.865
So in April

00:08:16.865 --> 00:08:17.745
2020,

00:08:18.465 --> 00:08:19.425
both the

00:08:19.985 --> 00:08:26.800
or at the end of March and the beginning of April, both the Board of Finance and the Board of Selectmen realized that

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:27.840
the

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.280
level of increases that they had been seeking to deal with some long term structural problems

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.760
just were not realistic or,

00:08:36.225 --> 00:08:37.505
you know, conscientable

00:08:37.505 --> 00:08:39.025
in this,

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:41.425
the midst of the COVID crisis.

00:08:41.665 --> 00:08:43.905
So I was asked as the administrator

00:08:44.065 --> 00:08:48.305
to trim the capital requests and the budgets as much as possible

00:08:48.465 --> 00:08:52.920
and get us down to as low a mill rate as we could go without,

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:58.120
you know, really dramatically affecting the way the town operates.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:02.600
So I submitted a revised budget to the Board of Selectmen,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.120
as well as a set of revised capital recommendations.

00:09:06.975 --> 00:09:11.215
And on April 13, the Board of Selectmen at their meeting

00:09:12.015 --> 00:09:15.135
made some further modifications to what I submitted

00:09:15.295 --> 00:09:16.095
and then

00:09:16.655 --> 00:09:17.775
passed that

00:09:18.015 --> 00:09:21.135
to the Board of Finance along with the timeline

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.730
of how the budget could be accomplished under the governor's You

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:28.210
just muted it.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:31.010
On April 27,

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:32.130
the

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.130
RAM board

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.450
adopted a final budget

00:09:37.695 --> 00:09:43.055
And in April also the Andover Elementary School came back to the town

00:09:43.375 --> 00:09:45.775
also with a revised budget.

00:09:47.055 --> 00:09:49.295
On Wednesday, April 28,

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:57.460
after a pretty extensive review of the budget and some changes of their own, the Board of Finance voted

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.180
to put a budget forth

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:01.220
to the public

00:10:02.285 --> 00:10:03.565
at this meeting.

00:10:04.685 --> 00:10:08.445
It's May 6 right now and this

00:10:08.605 --> 00:10:13.725
is the the Board of Finance's presentation to the town and to gather further feedback.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.340
So if we look at the kind of big picture of what our budget is

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:25.820
some of the nomenclature disappeared. Okay, the light green

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.220
is the elementary school budget.

00:10:28.635 --> 00:10:32.395
The elementary school budget represents about 31%

00:10:32.395 --> 00:10:34.315
of the town's total budget.

00:10:34.715 --> 00:10:37.995
The blue wedge is the debt remaining

00:10:38.155 --> 00:10:41.435
on the elementary school, that's about 1% of the budget.

00:10:41.755 --> 00:10:44.235
And yellow is the portion of the RAM budget,

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:46.770
which is about 42%.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.490
And everything else the town does,

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:51.250
including capital

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:53.170
expenditures,

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:54.930
is 26%

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.930
of the regular budget.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:04.205
And most of what we're going to talk about tonight is this part, because this is the part that's directly controlled by us.

00:11:05.645 --> 00:11:07.325
So the RAM budget,

00:11:07.485 --> 00:11:09.805
the governor made the

00:11:09.885 --> 00:11:18.200
RAM in the regional school districts do the same thing they made the towns do, which is they were ordered to adopt a budget,

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.000
you know, without going to public referendum.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.360
So RAM has adopted a budget,

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.160
which is basically a zero increase budget,

00:11:30.045 --> 00:11:30.685
but

00:11:31.245 --> 00:11:35.085
the effect on Andover is about a 6.8%

00:11:35.085 --> 00:11:35.965
increase

00:11:36.125 --> 00:11:38.925
in the RAM budget to Andover.

00:11:39.165 --> 00:11:41.965
And the reason is that next year we're sending

00:11:42.445 --> 00:11:44.205
more students to RAM

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:47.970
and the overall population of RAM is decreasing.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.410
So the total number of students is going down about 90,

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:56.290
but the number of Andover students is increasing next year by about 12.

00:11:56.945 --> 00:12:01.905
So our fraction of the total budget for RAM is going up 6.8%.

00:12:02.385 --> 00:12:06.065
And since that's our biggest single item in our budget,

00:12:06.385 --> 00:12:07.905
that represents

00:12:08.465 --> 00:12:14.790
quite a bit of money. So Andover's levy for the RAM budget is $5,200,000

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:18.550
and this is about a $337,000

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.430
increase

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:20.710
for this year.

00:12:22.975 --> 00:12:26.495
Elementary school budget, the Andover Board of Education

00:12:26.575 --> 00:12:28.975
requested a final budget of

00:12:29.295 --> 00:12:31.455
3,916,000.

00:12:32.095 --> 00:12:36.895
The Board of Finance voted to reduce that to 3,902,000,

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:43.290
which is a 4% decrease from last year's budget, or the current year's budget, I should say.

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:51.690
There is still debt service to be paid on the bond for the last expansion of the elementary school.

00:12:52.695 --> 00:12:55.255
That's $96,500

00:12:55.255 --> 00:12:57.735
and that debt will be retired

00:12:57.815 --> 00:12:59.735
in about two years.

00:13:01.895 --> 00:13:05.095
So when we look at town expenses,

00:13:05.655 --> 00:13:06.215
so

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:12.000
this gives you a pie chart which shows you basically the broad categories of expenses

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:13.520
that the town

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:14.640
has.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.135
Expenses related to the town hall include the cost of administration,

00:13:21.135 --> 00:13:22.815
myself and my assistant,

00:13:23.535 --> 00:13:25.295
tax collection assessment,

00:13:25.295 --> 00:13:27.215
town clerk, building department.

00:13:28.095 --> 00:13:29.615
That's about 24%

00:13:29.615 --> 00:13:31.055
of the total budget,

00:13:31.215 --> 00:13:32.895
but that doesn't include

00:13:33.295 --> 00:13:35.135
the employee benefits.

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:40.410
Public works and snow removal combined is about 10%

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:43.610
of the town's portion of the budget.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.650
Public safety is about 9%,

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:49.530
that is the fire department, as well as the

00:13:49.995 --> 00:13:54.155
resident state trooper, the fire marshal, animal control officer.

00:13:54.635 --> 00:13:57.195
The costs of running the transfer station

00:13:57.355 --> 00:13:59.915
are about 4% of the total budget.

00:14:01.035 --> 00:14:05.275
And then between senior services, youth services, and health services,

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.210
That's about 3% of the total budget.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:11.170
Overall employee benefits

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.050
are 11%,

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.690
town insurance is 3%,

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.890
the amount going into capital funds

00:14:20.695 --> 00:14:22.775
is about 21%,

00:14:23.095 --> 00:14:28.215
and all the little things that don't fit conveniently in one of those categories

00:14:28.375 --> 00:14:30.215
equate to about 13%

00:14:30.215 --> 00:14:31.415
of the overall budget.

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.980
So here's a budget summary which shows

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.580
for the three

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:40.980
major categories

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.380
what the budget was in

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:44.740
nineteen-twenty

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:45.620
versus

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.860
the proposed budget, the difference,

00:14:48.265 --> 00:14:54.825
and what the percentage is. And as you can see, the elementary school budget has decreased,

00:14:55.385 --> 00:14:57.625
the town budget has decreased,

00:14:58.025 --> 00:15:01.145
but the RAM budget, which is the biggest single thing we have,

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:04.880
has increased by about 6.9%

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:10.720
and therefore the total budget is still increasing about 2.8%.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:17.600
So the next thing we look at is kind of the final

00:15:16.695 --> 00:15:20.295
what effect does this have on the mill rate.

00:15:20.695 --> 00:15:23.975
So part of this is that the town

00:15:24.135 --> 00:15:24.935
is

00:15:25.335 --> 00:15:26.615
offsetting

00:15:26.615 --> 00:15:29.015
some of the capital expenses

00:15:29.255 --> 00:15:30.775
by reallocating

00:15:30.775 --> 00:15:31.895
existing funds,

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:34.650
as well as

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:39.610
using the money that's

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:41.930
in the unexpended fund balance.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.130
And so the total to be raised by taxation

00:15:45.875 --> 00:15:48.195
is about 9,000,004

00:15:48.195 --> 00:15:49.955
and $10,000

00:15:50.595 --> 00:15:53.075
And the other thing is that the

00:15:53.315 --> 00:15:56.675
grand list has increased a little over 1%,

00:15:56.995 --> 00:16:01.900
which means a mill is now worth about $264,000

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:08.300
And when you plug all that in, you get an estimated mill rate of 35.6,

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.860
which is an increase of about 1.75%.

00:16:14.255 --> 00:16:17.615
And that is it for the overview.

00:16:18.575 --> 00:16:23.695
So I will give up my screen sharing and I will turn this back over to Mark.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:27.150
>> Okay. Thanks, Eric.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:29.150
So

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:35.390
hopefully you were able to follow along there. Again, it's a high level overview. The details

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.510
have been very thoroughly gone through

00:16:38.885 --> 00:16:43.445
by the Board of Selectmen and also the Board of Finance, as well as Eric and his staff.

00:16:43.845 --> 00:16:46.405
I do want to make a couple of preliminary

00:16:46.405 --> 00:17:04.520
comments before we go to public speak. I think everyone understood, and hopefully it was clear from Eric's presentation, that the RAM budget is what it is. We cannot change that. We had a lot of feedback, and we did go through all of the feedback we got from the public in various venues. A lot of feedback about how the RAM budget was too high.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:07.640
Even a zero increased budget

00:17:08.325 --> 00:17:09.365
